 | `permission-<sessionId>` | the request it shows is replied to (`permission.replied`), or as above |
 
+Viewing a session (`POST /api/sessions/:id/view`, `clearSessionPushNotifications(id, { withSubagents: true })`) also clears the open notifications of its subagents (children and deeper) on the phones: a subagent's banner carries the subagent's own id, and the user reaches it through the parent. The session's own tags are cleared first. Then each other session with an open tag is walked up its parent chain, at most 8 steps, from the cache the walk itself fills or a 2 s read of its record (`fetchSessionParentId`); only the 50 newest distinct sessions with an open tag are considered, newest first. A chain that reaches the viewed session clears that session as a view would. A repeated id ends the walk (a cycle), and a session whose record cannot be read keeps its tags. Only the host's OpenCode is asked, since an open tag does not record a space. A message sent, a turn start and a settled request clear the session itself only.
+
 A `share-` tag survives the turn its own answer woke (`skipTurnStart`, see `notifyShareAnswer`). Pull request notifications have no cause the server can see handled, and stay until dismissed.
 
 The trigger runtime keeps the open tags (`openPushTags`: the session, the request a tag shows, whether it went to the phone apps, the id of its latest push) and the 20 most recently cleared ones, and saves both to `push-open-tags.json` in the data directory on every change, one write at a time, so a notification pushed before a restart is still cleared after it. One tag can show several requests in turn (two permissions of one session), so a reply clears the tag only when it answers the request the tag shows now. Clearing a tag also takes it out of the badge count, and the clearing push carries the new count.
